------\nGorenstein cut polytopes (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2013.11.010)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.5 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Normal cut polytopes ... Conjecture 1.2 ... Proof ... Then, the cut polytope Cut□⁢(𝐺) of 𝐺 is Gorenstein (i.e., 𝐾⁡[Cut□⁢(𝐺)] is normal and Gorenstein) if and only if 𝐺 has no 𝐾5-minor and satisfies one of the following:\nAn edge 𝑒 of 𝐺 is called a bridge if there exists no cycle of 𝐺 containing 𝑒. It is easy to see that a chordal graph 𝐺 has no bridges if and only if 𝐸⁡(𝐺) is the union of all triangles of 𝐺. A graph 𝐺 is called a bipartite graph if there exists a bipartition 𝑉⁡(𝐺) =𝑉1 ∪𝑉2 such that any edge of 𝐺 connects a vertex of 𝑉1 and a vertex of 𝑉2. It is known that 𝐺 is bipartite if and only if 𝐺 has no odd cycle. Let 𝑒 ={𝑖,𝑗} ∈𝐸⁡(𝐺) be an edge of 𝐺. Then, the new graph 𝐺 ∖𝑒 on the vertex set 𝑉⁡(𝐺) and the edge set 𝐸⁡(𝐺) ∖{𝑒} is called the graph obtained from 𝐺 by deleting the edge 𝑒. On the other hand, the new graph 𝐺⁡/𝑒 obtained by the procedure (i) Identify the vertices 𝑖 and 𝑗; (ii) Delete the multiple edges that may be created while (i); is called the graph obtained from 𝐺 by contracting the edge 𝑒. A graph 𝐻 is said to be a minor of 𝐺 if it can be obtained from 𝐺 by a sequence of deletions and/or contractions of edges (and deletions of vertices). Let 𝐾𝑛 denote the complete graph with 𝑛 vertices. It is known that\n\nProposition 1.1\n\n## [13]\n\nLet 𝐺 be a graph. If Cut□⁢(𝐺) is normal, then 𝐺 has no 𝐾5-minor.\n\nIt is conjectured that the converse of Proposition 1.1 holds in general.\n\nConjecture 1.2\n\n## [13]\n\nLet 𝐺 be a graph. Then, the following conditions are equivalent.\n\n  * (i)\n\nCut□⁢(𝐺) is normal.\n\n  * (ii)\n\n𝐾⁡[Cut□⁢(𝐺)] is Cohen–Macaulay.\n\n  * (iii)\n\n𝐺 has no 𝐾5-minor.\n\nAlthough the conjecture is still open, it is known that\n\nProposition 1.3\n\n## 2. Gorenstein cut polytopes are compressed\n\nIn this section, we show that any Gorenstein cut polytope is compressed. For the cut polytope 𝒫 =Cut□⁢(𝐺) ⊂ℝ𝑚 of 𝐺, let 𝑟⁢𝒫 ={𝑟⁢𝛼|𝛼∈𝒫} and let ℒ𝒫 be a sublattice of ℤ𝑚 spanned by the vertices of 𝒫. The relative interior of 𝒫 is denoted by int⁡(𝒫). The codegree of 𝒫 is codeg⁡(𝒫) =min⁡(𝑘∈ℕ|int⁡(𝑘⁢𝒫)∩ℒ𝒫≠0̸). Then, the following is known (see, e.g.,  [3]):\n\nProposition 2.1\n\nLet 𝒫 =Cut□⁢(𝐺) be the cut polytope of a graph 𝐺 with codegree 𝑑 . If 𝒫 is Gorenstein, then int⁡(𝑑⁢𝒫) ∩ℒ𝒫 ={𝐯} and any vector 𝐰 ∈int⁡((𝑑+𝑟)⁢𝒫) ∩ℒ𝒫 satisfies 𝐰 −𝐯 ∈𝑟⁢𝒫 ∩ℒ𝒫.\n\nSo, the following proposition  [8, p. 258] will play an important role.\n\nProposition 2.2\n\nLet 𝒫 =Cut□⁢(𝐺) be the cut polytope of a graph 𝐺 . Then, (𝑥1,…,𝑥𝑚) ∈ℒ𝒫 if and only if ∑𝑒𝑖∈𝐶𝑥𝑖 ≡0⁢mod⁡2 for each cycle 𝐶 of 𝐺.\n\nUsing these propositions, we have the following.\n\nTheorem 2.3\n\nIf the cut polytope Cut□⁢(𝐺) of a graph 𝐺 is Gorenstein, then Cut□⁢(𝐺) is compressed.\n\nProof\n\nSuppose that the cut polytope 𝒫 =Cut□⁢(𝐺) of a graph 𝐺 is Gorenstein and not compressed. Since 𝒫 is normal, 𝐺 has no 𝐾5-minor by Proposition 1.1. Thus, 𝐺 has an induced cycle of length ≥5 by Proposition 1.6. Let 𝑑 =codeg⁡(𝒫) and int⁡(𝑑⁢𝒫) ∩ℒ𝒫 ={𝐯=(𝑣1,…,𝑣𝑚)}. If 𝑥𝑖 =2 for all 1 ≤𝑖 ≤𝑚, then 0 <𝑥𝑖 <4 and 4⁢(|𝐹|−1)−∑𝑒𝑖∈𝐹𝑥𝑖+∑𝑒𝑗∈𝐶∖𝐹𝑥𝑗=2⁢(|𝐶|−2)>0 for any induced cycle 𝐶 and its odd subset 𝐹. By Proposition 1.4, Proposition 2.2, we have (2,…,2) ∈int⁡(4⁢𝒫) ∩ℒ𝒫. Thus, 2 ≤𝑑 ≤4 and 𝑣𝑖 ≤2 for all 𝑖 =1,2,…,𝑚 by Proposition 2.1.\n\nCase 1. There exists an induced even cycle 𝐶 of length ≥6.\n\nWe now come to the main result of the present paper.\n\nTheorem 3.4\n\nLet 𝐺 be a graph. Then, the cut polytope Cut□⁢(𝐺) of 𝐺 is Gorenstein (i.e., 𝐾⁡[Cut□⁢(𝐺)] is normal and Gorenstein) if and only if 𝐺 has no 𝐾5-minor and satisfies one of the following:\n\n  * (i)\n\n𝐺 is a bipartite graph without induced cycle of length ≥6 ;\n\n  * (ii)\n\n𝐺 is a bridgeless chordal graph.\n\nProof\n\n(⇒) Suppose that Cut□⁢(𝐺) is Gorenstein. By Theorem 2.3, Cut□⁢(𝐺) is compressed. Hence, 𝐺 has no 𝐾5-minor and no induced cycle of length ≥5. Since Cut□⁢(𝐺) is compressed and Gorenstein, Cut□⁢(𝐺) has a special simplex 𝛴 by Proposition 3.1.\n\nIf 𝐺 is bipartite, then 𝐺 has no induced cycle of length ≥6. If 𝐺 is not bipartite, then 𝐺 has an odd cycle. Since 𝐺 has no induced odd cycle of length ≥5, 𝐺 has a triangle. By Lemma 3.3, we have dim⁡𝛴 =3. Since dim⁡𝛴 ≠1, 𝐺 satisfies neither (a) nor (b) in Lemma 3.2. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA dichotomy for bounded degree graph homomorphisms with nonnegative weights (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcss.2022.09.002)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; We prove that the Bulatov-Grohe dichotomy [4] for ${Z}_{A}(\\cdot )$ holds for bounded degree graphs. ... For example, if $V(H)=\\{0,1\\}$ with an edge between 0 and 1 and a loop at 0, then $f:V(G)\\to \\{0,1\\}$ is a graph homomorphism iff ${f}^{-1}(1)$ is an independent set in G; similarly, proper vertex colorings on G using at most m colors correspond to homomorphisms from G to $H={K}_{m}$ (the complete graph on m vertices with no loops).\n\n## Journal of Computer and System Sciences\n\nDate: March 2023\n\nPages: 1-15\n\nVolume: Volume 132\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# A dichotomy for bounded degree graph homomorphisms with nonnegative weights☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Artem Govorov ^{a} ^{1} ^{2}, Jin-Yi Cai ^{a} ^{3}, Martin Dyer ^{b} ^{4}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jcss.2022.09.002\n\nAccess through your organizationView Open ManuscriptPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Cited by (2)\n  * Metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nEach symmetric matrix A defines a graph homomorphism function ${Z}_{A}(\\cdot )$, also known as the partition function. We prove that the Bulatov-Grohe dichotomy [4] for ${Z}_{A}(\\cdot )$ holds for bounded degree graphs. This resolves a problem that has been open for 15 years. Specifically, we prove that for any nonnegative symmetric matrix A with algebraic entries, either ${Z}_{A}(G)$ is in polynomial time for all graphs G, or it is #P-hard for bounded degree (and simple) graphs G. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGorenstein cut polytopes (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0195669813002795)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Although the conjecture is still open, it is known that ... Let G be a graph without {K}_{5}-minor. ... The normality of cut polytopes are preserved under 0, 1, 2-sum.\n## [13]\n\nLet G be a graph. Then, the following conditions are equivalent.\n\n  * (i)\n\nis normal.\n\n  * (ii)\n\nis Cohen–Macaulay.\n\n  * (iii)\n\nG has no {K}_{5}-minor.\n\nAlthough the conjecture is still open, it is known that\n\nProposition 1.3\n\n## [10]\n\nLet H be a minor of a graph G . If is normal, then so is .\n\nIn order to prove Proposition 1.3, the following Barahona–Mahjoub’s formula  [2] plays an important role.\n\nProposition 1.4\n\nLet G be a graph without {K}_{5}-minor. Then, is the solution set of the following linear inequalities:Moreover, each of them defines a facet of .\n\nLet and be graphs such that is a clique of both graphs. The new graph with the vertex set and edge set is called the clique sum of {G}_{1} and {G}_{2} along . If the cardinality of is , this operation is called a k-sum of the graphs. The normality of cut polytopes are preserved under 0, 1, 2-sum.\n\nProposition 1.5\n\n## [10]\n\nLet be a  0, 1  or  2-sum of {G}_{1} and {G}_{2} . Then, is normal if and only if both and are normal.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraph Homomorphisms with Complex Values: A Dichotomy Theorem (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/abs/10.1137/110840194)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; # Graph Homomorphisms with Complex Values: A Dichotomy Theorem ... Each symmetric matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ over $\\mathbb{C}$ defines a graph homomorphism function $Z_{\\bf A}(\\cdot)$ on undirected graphs. ... Bulatov, The complexity of the counting constraint satisfaction problem, in Proceedings of the 35th International Colloquium on Automata, Languages and Programming, 2008, pp.\n\n# Graph Homomorphisms with Complex Values: A Dichotomy Theorem\n\nAuthors: Jin-Yi Cai, Xi Chen, and Pinyan LuAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/110840194\n\n## Abstract\n\nEach symmetric matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ over $\\mathbb{C}$ defines a graph homomorphism function $Z_{\\bf A}(\\cdot)$ on undirected graphs. The function $Z_{\\mathbf{A}} (\\cdot)$ is also called the partition function from statistical physics, and can encode many interesting graph properties, including counting vertex covers and $k$-colorings. We study the computational complexity of $Z_{\\mathbf{A}} (\\cdot)$ for arbitrary symmetric matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ with algebraic complex values. Building on work by Dyer and Greenhill [Random Structures and Algorithms, 17 (2000), pp. 260--289], Bulatov and Grohe [Theoretical Computer Science, 348 (2005), pp. 148--186], and especially the recent beautiful work by Goldberg et al. [SIAM J. Comput., 39 (2010), pp. 3336--3402], we prove a complete dichotomy theorem for this problem. We show that $Z_{\\mathbf{A}} (\\cdot)$ is either computable in polynomial-time or \\#P-hard, depending explicitly on the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$. We further prove that the tractability criterion on $\\mathbf{A}$ is polynomial-time decidable.\n\n### Keywords\n\n  1. computational complexity\n  2. counting complexity\n  3. graph homomorphisms\n  4. partition functions\n\n### MSC codes\n\n  1. 68Q17\n  2. 68Q25\n  3. 68R05\n  4. 68R10\n  5. 05C31\n\n## References\n\n1.\n\nA. Bulatov, The complexity of the counting constraint satisfaction problem, in Proceedings of the 35th International Colloquium on Automata, Languages and Programming, 2008, pp. 646--661.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n---------------------------------------------------------